  <title id="bdl24deepkernelregression-title">Standard RBF-kernel GP versus a deep-kernel GP on a target with a sharp transition</title>
  <desc id="bdl24deepkernelregression-desc">Two panels share the same 60 noisy observations of f of x equal to tanh of 20 times (x minus 0.5) for x between 0 and 1, plotted for x from about minus 0.1 to 1.15 and f of x from minus 2 to 2. Left: a GP with a stationary RBF kernel on x follows the step but wiggles slightly along the flat parts, and its plus or minus two standard deviation band is narrow inside the data and widens sharply outside the training range, to roughly plus or minus 1 below x equal to 0 and up to about 1.9 above x equal to 1. Right: a GP with the same RBF kernel applied to a feature map that saturates away from the transition stays close to the target, and its band remains narrow everywhere, including outside the training range.</desc>
